## Why
`vector::at()` was called with index 5 on a vector of size 5, whose valid indexes are 0–4. It throws `std::out_of_range`, and since nothing catches it the program terminates. This is typically an off-by-one in a loop condition such as `i <= v.size()`.
## Fix
Stop the loop before the size.
```cpp
for (size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); i++) {
    total += v.at(i);
}
```

